# Dev mode

```sh
oriel dev
```

This starts the Vite dev server on `http://localhost:5173`, opens the app against it, and watches your Zig source files. When you edit `src/main.zig`, the app rebuilds and restarts automatically. The frontend hot-reloads. Dev builds use the app id with a `.Dev` suffix, so they run next to a production install without conflicts.

Press Ctrl-C to stop everything: the `dev_runner` manages Vite and the Zig app in a process group, so stopping the Zig build also stops the dev server.

# Type generation and `oriel types`

The Zig side defines `Commands` and `Events` as structs. The `oriel types` command regenerates the TypeScript types automatically:

```sh
oriel types
```

This reads your `Commands` and `Events` in `src/main.zig` and writes `frontend/src/oriel.ts`, which exports typed `invoke` and `listen`:

```ts
import { invoke, listen } from "./oriel";

const msg = await invoke("greet", { name: "Ada" });   // type-checked
const off = listen("notes_changed", (notes) => ...);  // type-checked
```

If you add a new command `pub fn export_data(...)` to `Commands`, run `oriel types` and the TypeScript import gains the new function.

# Type checking with `oriel check`

Without building the full app:

```sh
oriel check
```

This type-checks your Zig code in about a second, using `zig build check`. Handy while iterating.

# Logging

The app logs to stderr and to a file in the data directory:

```sh
$ cat ~/.local/share/<app_id>/app.log
```

In `src/main.zig`, use standard Zig logging:

```zig
std.log.info("Note added: {s}", .{text});
std.log.err("Database error: {s}", .{@errorName(err)});
```

Scoped logging by module:

```zig
const log = std.log.scoped(.notes);
log.debug("Loading notes", .{});
```

In debug and dev builds, JavaScript `console.log()`, `console.error()`, etc. are forwarded to stdout, so you can monitor them alongside the Zig logs.

# Testing GUI apps headlessly

For CI or automated testing, run the app without a real display:

```sh
scripts/headless.sh ./zig-out/bin/my-app --auto-quit
```

This launches a virtual X11 display (Xvfb) and a private D-Bus session, runs your app, and exits when the app quits. The webview and tray work normally; use `--auto-quit` if your app can't close itself (pass it to your app).

Take a screenshot after a delay:

```sh
SHOT=/tmp/shot.png scripts/headless.sh ./zig-out/bin/my-app
# app runs for ~4 s, screenshot saved to /tmp/shot.png
```

This is how the [examples/smoke]($link.page('docs/modules')) test runs on every push.

# Debugging tips

## Zig panics and backtraces

In a debug or dev build, panics print a stack trace. Set the environment variable to get more detail:

```sh
ZIG_BACKTRACE=1 oriel dev
```

## WebView debug mode

In debug builds, the WebKit inspector is enabled. On Linux (X11), press F12 to open it (Wayland: check your desktop environment's keybindings).

## GTK debug output

For GTK-specific issues:

```sh
G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all oriel dev 2>&1 | grep -i gtk
```

## Tray menu introspection

Inside a headless session, query the tray menu structure via D-Bus:

```sh
gdbus call --session --dest org.kde.StatusNotifierItem-$PID-1 --object-path /MenuBar \
  --method com.canonical.dbusmenu.GetLayout 0 -- -1 '[]'
```

Replace `$PID` with the app's process ID.

## Memory and threads

Oriel's platform layer is designed for memory safety. The framework uses:
- `defer` and `errdefer` for cleanup on all error paths.
- Thread-safe command dispatch (async commands run on a worker pool, replies go back on the main thread).
- No manual pointer casting except where justified (see PLAN.md rules).

If you see unexpected crashes or memory issues, check:
1. All SQL prepared statements are finalized.
2. All allocated memory is freed or returned to the caller (document ownership).
3. Events and commands receive allocated data (not stack or temporary allocations).
4. The app doesn't block the main thread (use async commands for I/O or slow operations).
